system "ALA-LC:zho-Hani:Latn:2012" {

metadata {
  authority_id alalc
  id 2012
  language iso-639-2:zho
  source_script Hani
  destination_script Latn
  name "Chinese Romanization Table (2012)"
  url https://www.loc.gov/catdir/cpso/romanization/chinese.pdf
  creation_date 2012
  adoption_date
  description {    ALA-LC Chinese Romanization Table, 2012 version. The
    2012 table follows the principles of the Pinyin system, replacing
    the Wade-Giles system of earlier editions, and does not indicate
    tone marks (Rules of Application 1). Separation of Syllables: the
    romanization of each Chinese character is separated by a space.
    This map encodes the character readings attested by the document's
    own examples, output as lowercase syllables separated by spaces,
    and the deterministic punctuation equivalents. Transcribed from the
    loc.gov document's complete text layer. }

  notes {
    note "Character coverage: the document provides no general
    hanzi-to-pinyin table - Rules of Application 2 delegate readings to
    Ci hai 辞海 and other dictionaries. The character table here is the
    set of 321 distinct characters attested with readings in the
    document's own example lines; both simplified and traditional forms
    are mapped as attested. The companion map alalc-zho-Latn-Latn-2012
    encodes the document's complete Wade-Giles to Pinyin correspondence
    table (pages 9-21)."
    note "Multiple readings: 大 is attested both as dai (韋大夫 | Wei
    dai fu) and da (智顗大師 | Zhiyi da shi; 中山大學 | Zhongshan da
    xue; 长江大饭店 | Chang Jiang da fan dian; 泸州长江大桥 | Luzhou
    Changjiang Daqiao) and maps to any([dai, da]). No other character
    carries two readings across the document's examples."
    note "Rules recorded but not encoded (word formation above the
    character level, requiring name/title segmentation): joining the
    syllables of multi-character surnames, given names, geographic
    names and jurisdictions (Connection of syllables 1, 1A-1E, 2,
    2A-2J, 3); apostrophe insertion before joined vowel-initial
    syllables in cases of ambiguity (Connection of syllables 4, e.g.
    Chang'an Shi vs Changan Shi); capitalization (Capitalization 1-4;
    titles and terms of address lower-cased, e.g. Lin lao shi); the
    date rules joining reign periods (Dates 1, e.g. Guangxu ji chou);
    systematic romanization of non-Chinese-origin words (Rules of
    Application 3)."
    note "Punctuation rules recorded but not encoded: the centered
    point (•) transcribes as a comma between coordinate words and as a
    space between personal-name elements (Punctuation 1) - the choice
    is context-dependent. The bracket rule (Punctuation 2) is
    deterministic and is encoded: 「」『』《》 romanize as quotation
    marks."
  }
}

tests {
  # Document examples reproducible end-to-end under the Separation of
  # Syllables rule (each character's syllable separated by a space;
  # common-noun/direction examples printed lowercase in the document).
   "西北" ->  "xi bei"
   "东北" ->  "dong bei"
   "東北" ->  "dong bei"  # 2I: direction/position reading
   "一九九八年" ->  "yi jiu jiu ba nian"
   "一九九零年" ->  "yi jiu jiu ling nian"
  # Single-character entries (readings attested in the document's
  # examples; one or more per romanization initial, plus traditional
  # forms and special rows).
   "安" ->  "an"
   "昂" ->  "ang"
   "北" ->  "bei"
   "博" ->  "bo"
